                <h2>Start with the Definition: Delivery Does Not Equal Sales</h2>
<p>The most common mistake in a Meta creative review is treating “the platform continues to distribute the ad” as “customers are willing to buy.” An ad may scale because it receives impressions, clicks, or stable conversion events. Whether it can actually sell requires a further assessment of order quality, acquisition cost, retention, and profit.</p>
<p>Classify each creative into one of four states:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Not scaling:</strong> Delivery is restricted, impressions remain low, or creative fatigue appears quickly.</li>
<li><strong>Generating clicks:</strong> Click-through rate is acceptable, but landing-page sessions rarely convert.</li>
<li><strong>Converting:</strong> The ad generates purchases or qualified leads, but the cost remains above target.</li>
<li><strong>Profitable and scalable:</strong> Conversion quality remains stable, new-customer contribution margin is positive, and efficiency does not deteriorate materially as budget increases.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Pre-Launch Checklist: Rule Out Tracking Issues First</h2>
<p>Before judging creative performance, verify the following:</p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Purchase events are firing correctly, Pixel and Conversions API are deduplicated, and transaction values and product information are included.</li>
<li>[ ] The campaign optimization event matches the current objective and is not mixing results from old retargeting creatives.</li>
<li>[ ] Product details, pricing, inventory, delivery time, and the landing page match the ad promise.</li>
<li>[ ] Geography, language, device, and audience settings match the current business stage.</li>
<li>[ ] Attribution windows align with the actual sales cycle so reported ROAS is not misleading.</li>
<li>[ ] Every creative has a unique ID, with audience, placement, budget, and launch date recorded.</li>
</ul>
<p>If clicks increase while landing-page bounce rate rises at the same time, check the mismatch between the ad promise and the page before blaming the copy.</p>
<h2>Diagnose the Creative Layer by Layer</h2>
<h3>1. Evaluate the Opening Hook</h3>
<p>Review the three-second view rate, video retention, outbound clicks, and conversions. An opening that only displays the product without showing a use case, customer pain point, or outcome may attract broad traffic while failing to identify high-intent users.</p>
<p><strong>Checks:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Does the first line state who the product helps and what problem it solves?</li>
<li>Is the visual clear on mobile, with readable captions?</li>
<li>Does the CTA match the customer’s current stage?</li>
<li>Are there unrelated exaggerated claims or terms that could create a policy risk?</li>
</ul>
<h3>2. Assess Post-Click Traffic Quality</h3>
<p>Do not rely on CTR alone. Connect ad clicks to landing-page sessions, add-to-cart events, and checkout starts using the same creative ID. Watch for these patterns:</p>
<ul>
<li>High CTR with high bounce rate: the creative is attracting the wrong clicks.</li>
<li>Adequate traffic but low add-to-cart rate: pricing, trust signals, or product-market fit may be weak.</li>
<li>Healthy add-to-cart rate but low payment completion: checkout usability, payment options, shipping charges, or inventory may be blocking the purchase.</li>
<li>Normal order volume with high refunds or cancellations: the creative may overpromise or attract low-quality buyers.</li>
</ul>
<h3>3. Determine Whether the Demand Is Incremental</h3>
<p>Separate Meta-driven orders from organic and brand-search orders. If most Meta orders would have occurred anyway, the campaign may be harvesting existing demand rather than creating new demand. Evaluate incrementality using region-level trends, new-customer share, brand-search behavior, and changes before and after launch.</p>
<p>For a utility app, monitor activation-to-key-action conversion, trial-to-paid conversion, and D7 retention. For a cross-border e-commerce brand, review new-customer share, average order value, refund rate, and recognized contribution margin.</p>
<h2>Converting Does Not Mean Scalable</h2>
<p>Increase budget only when the creative meets the following conditions:</p>
<ul>
<li>The conversion sample is large enough for a reliable decision rather than relying on one isolated order.</li>
<li>Acquisition cost remains within the target range over a sustained period.</li>
<li>New-customer revenue or first-order contribution margin covers acquisition spend.</li>
<li>Daily budgets are not repeatedly capped, audiences are not excessively narrow, and the learning phase is not resetting too often.</li>
<li>A 20%–30% budget increase does not cause marginal costs to deteriorate continuously.</li>
</ul>
<p>If a creative converts but loses money, improve audience targeting, offer structure, landing pages, or incentive design before deciding whether to retain it. Do not hide weak margin by optimizing for a lower CPI alone.</p>
<h2>Weekly Review SOP: Scale, Iterate, Monitor, or Stop</h2>
<p>Complete a creative-level review every week:</p>
<ol>
<li>Exclude approval restrictions, tracking failures, and inventory changes.</li>
<li>Compare opening metrics, click quality, funnel conversion, and order quality.</li>
<li>Monitor frequency, seven-day cost changes, and signs of creative fatigue.</li>
<li>Label every creative as “scale,” “iterate,” “monitor,” or “stop,” and document the reason.</li>
<li>Change only one key variable during iteration—such as the opening hook, evidence order, or CTA—so the result remains interpretable.</li>
</ol>
<blockquote>
<p>A strong Meta creative review is not about finding the lowest CPI. It is about proving that every budget increment is connected to qualified orders and profitable growth.</p>
</blockquote>
